From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Mower <mdmower@cmphys.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:11:15 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] [bugs #3703] sdas: reserve relocation buffer space for ACALL
 and AJMP to an absolute address

For an ACALL or AJMP to an absolute address, outrwm() and outr3bm(), which write the 11-bit and the 19-bit form, called outchk() with a relocation size of 0 and then wrote four bytes to rel[]. outchk() therefore did not begin a new T line when fewer than four of the 16 bytes of rel[] were free, and the bytes after rel[] were overwritten. Where txt[] follows rel[], the T line then began with FF FF in place of its address. For the file in the ticket, sdld reported relocation errors for that line and ended with a segmentation fault.

Both functions now reserve five bytes, as the other callers that write a relocation with write_rmode() and out_rw() do.

The ticket shows another symptom for the same file in a build of 4.3.0, a location counter of FF808000 after the ACALL. What follows rel[] in memory depends on the build, and that build was not examined.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sdas/asxxsrc/asout.c                |  4 ++--
 support/regression/tests/bug-3703.c | 37 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 39 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 support/regression/tests/bug-3703.c

diff --git a/sdas/asxxsrc/asout.c b/sdas/asxxsrc/asout.c
index 17707d56a..09699fe92 100644
--- a/sdas/asxxsrc/asout.c
+++ b/sdas/asxxsrc/asout.c
@@ -1976,7 +1976,7 @@ outrwm(struct expr *esp, int r, a_uint v)
                                  */
                                 out_lw(esp->e_addr,0);
                                 if (oflag) {
-                                        outchk(3, 0);
+                                        outchk(3, 5);
                                         out_txb(2, esp->e_addr);
                                         *txtp++ = v;
 
@@ -2029,7 +2029,7 @@ outr3bm(struct expr * esp, int r, a_uint v)
 			 */
                         out_lw(esp->e_addr,0);
 			if (oflag) {
-                                outchk(4, 0);
+                                outchk(4, 5);
                                 out_txb(3, esp->e_addr);
                                 *txtp++ = v;
 
diff --git a/support/regression/tests/bug-3703.c b/support/regression/tests/bug-3703.c
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..2c70cfbfb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/support/regression/tests/bug-3703.c
@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+/* bug-3703.c
+   sdas8051 wrote the relocation of an acall or ajmp to an absolute address
+   past the end of its relocation buffer when fewer than four bytes of the
+   buffer were free. Where the text buffer follows the relocation buffer in
+   memory, the T line in the object file then had a wrong address.
+ */
+
+#include <testfwk.h>
+
+#ifdef __SDCC_mcs51
+__data unsigned char d;
+extern const __code unsigned char insns[];
+
+/* This function is never called. The relocation buffer of the assembler is
+   empty at the label. The two mov instructions leave two of its 16 bytes
+   free, and the acall needs four. The linker accepts the address 0x0123
+   because it places this code in the first 2K page. */
+void
+overrun (void) __naked
+{
+  __asm
+_insns::
+	mov	a, _d
+	mov	a, _d
+	acall	0x0123
+  __endasm;
+}
+#endif
+
+void
+testBug (void)
+{
+#ifdef __SDCC_mcs51
+  ASSERT (insns[4] == 0x31);
+  ASSERT (insns[5] == 0x23);
+#endif
+}
